I have gathered all the relevant code. Here is the complete picture of how owner address data is handled in this repository (a Spring PetClinic REST variant with an OfficeFloor function pipeline; DTOs are generated from `openapi.yml`, not hand-written `OwnerRequest`/`OwnerResponse` classes).

---

## 1. Request/DTO with an `address` field

There is no `OwnerRequest` class. The request body type is the generated `OwnerFieldsDto`, defined by the `OwnerFields` schema in the OpenAPI spec.

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml` (lines 1991-2070), schema `OwnerFields`. Relevant `address` field:

```yaml
address:
  title: Address
  description: The postal address of the pet owner.
  type: string
  minLength: 1
  maxLength: 255
  example: '110 W. Liberty St.'
```
Also declares `city` (maxLength 80), `telephone`, `email`, `sharesHousehold` (writeOnly boolean), and `postcode` (`pattern: '^[0-9]{4}$'`). Required: `firstName, lastName, address, city, telephone`. Note: only a flat `address` string exists — there is NO `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` field in the current schema.

The request DTO is consumed in these functions:

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/ValidateOwner.java` — bean-validates the `@Valid @RequestBody OwnerFieldsDto` before load (full source above; just passes the validated request through).
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/BuildOwner.java` — builds an `Owner` from the request on create (full source shown). Key address handling: `String address = AddressNormalizer.normalize(request.getAddress());` then requires it non-blank and `owner.setAddress(address)`.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/ApplyOwner.java` — applies request fields on update:
```java
public void service(@Val Owner owner, @Val OwnerFieldsDto request) {
    owner.setAddress(request.getAddress());   // NOTE: NOT normalized here (unlike BuildOwner)
    owner.setCity(request.getCity());
    ...
}
```
Note the inconsistency: update path (`ApplyOwner`) stores the raw address, while the create path (`BuildOwner`) normalizes it. `ApplyOwner` also does not set `postcode`.

The domain model `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java` holds `address`, `city`, `postcode`, `householdId`, `customerCode`, etc. `address` and `city` are `@NotEmpty`. (Full source read; `setEmail` lower-cases email — its Javadoc misleadingly says "Stores the address lower-cased".)

