```
The thrown exception type is `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/MissingOwnerFieldsException.java` (handled as 400 by `MissingOwnerFieldsHandler.java`).

---

## 3. Household hash, postcode validation, locality

Household hash — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignHouseholdId.java` (full source):

```java
public void service(@Val Owner owner) {
    String postcode = owner.getPostcode();
    String key = CheckUniqueHousehold.normalize(owner.getLastName())
            + "|" + (postcode == null ? "" : postcode);
    owner.setHouseholdId(hash(key));
}
// First 6 bytes (12 hex chars) of SHA-256(key), lower-case hex
static String hash(String key) { ... 6 bytes -> 12 hex chars ... }
```
The hash is derived from normalized `lastName` + `postcode` (NOT the address string, despite some OpenAPI descriptions mentioning "same lastName and address").

Household uniqueness/tier:
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/CheckUniqueHousehold.java` — rejects duplicate `householdId` unless `sharesHousehold=true`; contains the shared `normalize(value)` helper (trim, collapse whitespace, lower-case). Full source above. Throws `DuplicateHouseholdException` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/DuplicateHouseholdException.java`, 409 via `DuplicateHouseholdHandler.java`).
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/HouseholdTier.java` — `isGold` when 3+ owners share a `householdId`. Full source above.

Postcode validation — `ValidatePostcode.java` (see section 2).

Locality — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/Locality.java` (full source):

```java
private static final Map<String, String> CITY_REGION = Map.of(
        "Sydney", "NSW", "Melbourne", "VIC", "Brisbane", "QLD");

public static String locality(Owner owner) {   // REGION segment of customerCode "<REGION>-<HASH8>"
    String code = owner.getCustomerCode();
    int dash = code == null ? -1 : code.indexOf('-');
    return dash > 0 ? code.substring(0, dash) : region(owner);
}
public static String region(Owner owner) {      // postcode range wins, else city table, else "UNKNOWN"
    String byPostcode = byPostcode(owner.getPostcode());
    return byPostcode != null ? byPostcode : CITY_REGION.getOrDefault(owner.getCity(), "UNKNOWN");
}
private static String byPostcode(String postcode) { ... 2000-2099 NSW / 3000-3099 VIC / 4000-4099 QLD ... }
```
Related: `AssignCustomerCode.java` builds `customerCode` as `<REGION>-<HASH8>` using `Locality.region(owner)` (full source above), and `Timezone.of(...)` maps region -> IANA tz (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/Timezone.java`). `FlagPossibleDuplicate.java` uses `postcode` + normalized `lastName` to detect soft duplicates (full source above).

---

## 4. Address normalization logic

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AddressNormalizer.java` (full source):

```java
public final class AddressNormalizer {
    private static final Map<String, String> ABBREV = Map.of("ST", "STREET", "RD", "ROAD", "AVE", "AVENUE");
    private AddressNormalizer() {}
    public static String normalize(String value) {
        if (value == null) return "";
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (String word : value.trim().toUpperCase().split("\\s+")) {
            if (word.isEmpty()) continue;
            if (sb.length() > 0) sb.append(' ');
            sb.append(ABBREV.getOrDefault(word, word));
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```
Trims, collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands `ST->STREET, RD->ROAD, AVE->AVENUE`. Only invoked from `BuildOwner.service` (create). It is NOT called from `ApplyOwner` (update). Related normalizers: `CheckUniqueHousehold.normalize` (lastName), `Owner.setEmail` (lower-case), `BuildOwner.toE164` (telephone).

---

## 5. Tests related to owners / address / validation / household / postcode / locality

Only acceptance (black-box, MockMvc) tests exist — there are no unit tests for the functions above.

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` (full source shown). Directly targets address normalization and structured address:
  - `coreNormalizesAndComposesStructuredAddress` posts `addressLine1: "10 king st"` (removes `addressLine2`) and expects `$.addressLine1 == "10 KING STREET"` and `$.address == "10 KING STREET"`.
  - `functionalityStillAcceptsFlatAddress` posts a flat `address` owner and expects 2xx.
  - Important: this test expects STRUCTURED address fields (`addressLine1`/`addressLine2`) on both request and response, but the current `OwnerFields`/`Owner` OpenAPI schema, `Owner` model, and mapper only support a flat `address`. So `coreNormalizesAndComposesStructuredAddress` describes an unimplemented feature and would currently fail (the structured input is dropped, `address` becomes blank/normalized-empty).

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` (full source shown). Provides address/postcode/household/locality test scaffolding: `uniqueAddress()`, `withPostcode()`, `structuredOwner()` (uses `addressLine1` + `postcode`), pinned reference tables `CITY_REGION`, `REGION_TIMEZONE`, `REGION_POSTCODES`, helpers `region()/timezone()/validPostcode()/knownOwner()`, and `sha256hex`/`shaHex`/`luhn` to replicate the household-hash and customer-code algorithms.

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AuditLogCapture.java` — audit-log test helper for owner creation (not address-specific).

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/resources/application.properties` — test config only.

---

## 6. Response DTO returning an `address` string

There is no `OwnerResponse` class. The response type is the generated `OwnerDto` (OpenAPI `Owner` schema, lines 2071-2214 of `openapi.yml`), which via `allOf` inherits all `OwnerFields` properties including the flat `address` string, and adds read-only derived fields: `displayName, initials, telephoneDisplay, customerCode, membershipNumber, householdId, identityKey, namesakeCount, membershipPoints, membershipLevel, locality, timezone, contactPreference, ageBand, bulkSignupWarning, checkDigit, possibleDuplicate, possibleDuplicateOf, pets`. There is no `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` in the response schema.

Response construction:
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/RespondWithOwner.java` — `response.send(ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner))` (full source above). Companions: `RespondWithOwnerCreated.java`, `RespondWithOwnerUpdated.java`.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java` (full source above) — MapStruct mapper `toOwnerDto`. `address` maps straight through; derived fields via expressions, e.g. `locality` -> `Locality.locality(owner)`, `timezone` -> `Timezone.of(Locality.locality(owner))`. It has no explicit `address` mapping/transformation, so whatever is stored on the `Owner` is returned verbatim.

---

## Key cross-cutting observations

- The household hash (`AssignHouseholdId`) and postcode validation (`ValidatePostcode`) key off `lastName` + `postcode` + `city`, NOT the free-text `address`, even though several OpenAPI/Javadoc descriptions say "same lastName and address."
- Address normalization (`AddressNormalizer`, incl. `ST->STREET`) runs only on create (`BuildOwner`), not on update (`ApplyOwner`), so the two paths persist address differently.
- The acceptance tests assume a STRUCTURED address model (`addressLine1`/`addressLine2`) and a composed normalized `address`, none of which is present in the current DTO/model/mapper — this is the gap between spec (tests) and implementation.

