There is no `OwnerResponse` class. The response type is the generated `OwnerDto` (OpenAPI `Owner` schema, lines 2071-2214 of `openapi.yml`), which via `allOf` inherits all `OwnerFields` properties including the flat `address` string, and adds read-only derived fields: `displayName, initials, telephoneDisplay, customerCode, membershipNumber, householdId, identityKey, namesakeCount, membershipPoints, membershipLevel, locality, timezone, contactPreference, ageBand, bulkSignupWarning, checkDigit, possibleDuplicate, possibleDuplicateOf, pets`. There is no `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` in the response schema.

Response construction:
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/RespondWithOwner.java` — `response.send(ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner))` (full source above). Companions: `RespondWithOwnerCreated.java`, `RespondWithOwnerUpdated.java`.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java` (full source above) — MapStruct mapper `toOwnerDto`. `address` maps straight through; derived fields via expressions, e.g. `locality` -> `Locality.locality(owner)`, `timezone` -> `Timezone.of(Locality.locality(owner))`. It has no explicit `address` mapping/transformation, so whatever is stored on the `Owner` is returned verbatim.

---

## Key cross-cutting observations

- The household hash (`AssignHouseholdId`) and postcode validation (`ValidatePostcode`) key off `lastName` + `postcode` + `city`, NOT the free-text `address`, even though several OpenAPI/Javadoc descriptions say "same lastName and address."
- Address normalization (`AddressNormalizer`, incl. `ST->STREET`) runs only on create (`BuildOwner`), not on update (`ApplyOwner`), so the two paths persist address differently.
- The acceptance tests assume a STRUCTURED address model (`addressLine1`/`addressLine2`) and a composed normalized `address`, none of which is present in the current DTO/model/mapper — this is the gap between spec (tests) and implementation.

